<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Graves Simcoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1752">1752</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Graves Simcoe was a British army officer who commanded the Queen's Rangers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. As the first lieutenant governor of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> from 1791 to 1796 he encouraged Loyalist settlement, laid out roads such as Yonge Street and moved the capital from Newark to York, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>. He also banned the import of slaves in 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-graves-simcoe.jpg" alt="John Graves Simcoe" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Theodore Berthon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ColonelSimcoe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Simcoe was born in Cotterstock on 25 February 1752, the only surviving son of John Simcoe, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> captain who commanded HMS Pembroke at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> as his sailing master, and who died in 1759 before the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>. The family then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter-new-hampshire">Exeter</a>. Simcoe was educated at Exeter Grammar School and Eton College and spent a year at Merton College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, before choosing the army. He joined the 35th Regiment of Foot as an ensign in 1770.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sent to the Thirteen Colonies in 1775, he served at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> and, as a captain of the 40th Regiment of Foot, was wounded at the Battle of Brandywine on 11 September 1777. That year he took command of the Queen's Rangers, which fought at Crooked Billet and Monmouth and defeated Daniel Nimham's Stockbridge Militia at Kingsbridge in 1778. Captured in 1779 and released at the end of the year, he joined Benedict Arnold's raid on Richmond and was taken again at Yorktown. In 1782 he married Elizabeth Posthuma Gwillim, and as Member of Parliament for St Mawes from 1790 he spoke against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><p>Appointed lieutenant governor on 12 September 1791, he reached Kingston on 24 June 1792. Prompted by the Chloe Cooley incident, and working with his Attorney-General John White, he secured the Act Against Slavery of 1793, the first legislation to limit slavery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>: it also freed children born to enslaved women when they reached 25. Soldiers of the Queen's Rangers began cutting Yonge Street in August 1793, and he also began Dundas Street between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, a place he had named. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northwest-indian-war">Northwest Indian War</a> he hoped for an Indian barrier state and built Fort Miami, but after the Jay Treaty he evacuated the frontier forts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Simcoe returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in July 1796, suffering from gout. He was governor of British-occupied Saint-Domingue from October 1796 until March 1797, when British forces faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toussaint-louverture">Toussaint Louverture</a>'s troops. Appointed Commander-in-Chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in 1806, he fell ill on the way and died in Exeter on 26 October, and was buried at Wolford Chapel near Honiton. Lake Simcoe, named after his father, and Simcoe Day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> keep his name, and his regiment survives as the Queen's York Rangers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Graves Simcoe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its first lieutenant governor, <b>John Graves Simcoe</b>, laid out roads and moved the capital from Newark to York, the future Toronto, in 1796.</blockquote><a href="/state/upper-canada">Upper Canada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cotterstock, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.5,-0.47&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Cotterstock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Graves Simcoe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.5,-0.47&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
